Memory Allocation 
You may sometimes need to allocate memory in an extension that won't be used for 
object storage---perhaps you've got a giant bitmap for a Bloom filter, or an image, 
or a whole bunch of little structures that Ruby doesn't use directly. 
In order to work correctly with the garbage collector, you should use the following 
memory allocation routines. These routines do a little bit more work than the 
standard malloc. For instance, if ALLOC_N determines that it cannot allocate the 
desired amount of memory, it will invoke the garbage collector to try to reclaim 
some space. It will raise a NoMemError if it can't or if the requested amount of 
memory is invalid. 






